            "transcript": "In April 2026, NASA launched the Artemis II mission, marking humanity's first journey to the vicinity of the Moon since Apollo 17 in 1972. Four astronauts — Commander Reid Wiseman, Pilot Victor Glover, Mission Specialist Christina Koch, and Canadian astronaut Jeremy Hansen — boarded the Orion spacecraft, propelled by the powerful Space Launch System rocket, for an approximately ten-day lunar flyby.\n\nThe mission represented a critical step in testing deep-space operations. The Orion spacecraft traveled to about 10,000 kilometers beyond the far side of the Moon, where the crew evaluated the performance of life support systems, communication equipment, and navigation instruments under real deep-space conditions. One remarkable moment came when the astronauts conducted the first-ever live video broadcast from deep space, with over 520 million viewers worldwide watching in real time.\n\nHowever, the mission was not without challenges. On the third day of the flight, the spacecraft's solar panels experienced a brief power fluctuation that caused temporary concern at mission control. Engineers quickly identified the issue as a calibration anomaly and successfully resolved it by remotely adjusting the panel orientation, restoring full power within two hours.\n\nThe Artemis II mission concluded safely, and its success has paved the way for Artemis III, planned for 2027, which aims to land astronauts on the lunar surface for the first time in over five decades. NASA Administrator Bill Nelson called Artemis II \"a turning point in human space exploration,\" adding that it proves we are ready to return to the Moon — and eventually reach Mars.\n\nQuestion 15: How many astronauts were aboard the Artemis II mission?\nQuestion 16: How far did the Orion spacecraft travel beyond the far side of the Moon?\nQuestion 17: What technical issue occurred during the mission?",
