        "title": "The 2026 World AI Conference and the Rise of \"Baiyuzhi\"",
        "passage": "The 2026 World Artificial Intelligence Conference (WAIC 2026), held in Shanghai from July 6 to 8, drew over 200,000 attendees and featured more than 1,500 exhibitors from 62 countries. Under the theme \"Intelligent Connectivity, Infinite Possibilities,\" the conference showcased breakthroughs that are rapidly moving AI from laboratories into everyday life.\n\nThe undisputed highlight of WAIC 2026 was the official launch of \"Baiyuzhi\" (百域智), China's most ambitious general-purpose AI platform to date. Developed by a consortium led by the Shanghai AI Laboratory in collaboration with 18 universities and 12 tech companies, Baiyuzhi integrates natural language processing, computer vision, and reasoning capabilities into a single unified architecture. Unlike previous AI models that excelled in only one domain, Baiyuzhi demonstrated competence across 147 different task categories during its live demonstration, including medical image analysis, legal document drafting, and real-time multilingual interpretation.\n\nWhat sets Baiyuzhi apart is its \"domain-aware\" design. The platform can automatically detect the professional context of a user's query and adjust its reasoning framework accordingly. For instance, when asked the same medical question, Baiyuzhi provides a general explanation to a layperson but offers detailed diagnostic reasoning when it identifies the user as a healthcare professional. This adaptive capability earned Baiyuzhi the conference's top innovation award.\n\nIn the healthcare sector, several AI applications drew significant attention. A system called \"HealthPal,\" developed by researchers at Tsinghua University, demonstrated the ability to detect early signs of depression and anxiety by analyzing subtle patterns in users' speech and facial micro-expressions. Clinical trials involving 12,000 participants reported an accuracy rate of 89%, surpassing most traditional screening methods. The developers emphasized that HealthPal is intended as a preliminary screening tool to assist—not replace—mental health professionals.\n\nThe conference also addressed pressing ethical concerns. China's Ministry of Science and Technology released the \"White Paper on AI Ethics Governance (2026),\" which established guidelines for algorithmic transparency, data privacy protection, and accountability in AI decision-making. The white paper introduced a mandatory \"AI impact assessment\" for any AI system deployed in critical sectors such as healthcare, criminal justice, and education. Conference chair Professor Zhang Wei noted, \"We are entering an era where governing AI wisely is just as important as developing it rapidly.\" 28.  What distinguished Baiyuzhi from previous AI models?",
