        "passage": "The year 2026 has proven to be a watershed moment for China's aerospace ambitions, marked by two landmark achievements that signal the country's growing capabilities in space technology: the successful sea-based recovery of a reusable rocket and the inauguration of the \"Space Computing Constellation.\"\n\nOn May 24, 2026, China Aerospace Science and Technology Corporation (CASC. achieved a historic milestone when the Long March 10 rocket's first-stage booster successfully executed a controlled descent and precision landing onto an unmanned recovery vessel stationed in the South China Sea. The booster, having delivered its payload into low Earth orbit, re-entered the atmosphere and used grid fins and engine re-ignition to guide itself to the designated landing zone—a rectangular platform measuring just 52 by 38 meters on the drone ship \"Haijian-02.\" This achievement makes China only the second nation, after the United States, to master the complex technology of large- scale rocket recovery at sea.\n\nThe significance of this breakthrough extends far beyond national pride. Reusable rocket technology has the potential to reduce launch costs by an estimated 60 to 70 percent, dramatically lowering the economic barrier to space access. CASC officials announced plans to conduct at least six additional recovery tests through 2027, with the goal of achieving full reusability for the Long March 10 by 2028. The technology is expected to play a crucial role in China's upcoming lunar missions, including the Chang'e-7 south pole exploration mission and the construction of the International Lunar Research Station.\n\nEqually ambitious is the \"Space Computing Constellation\" (太空计算星座), whose first experimental satellite, Tiansuan-1 (天算一号), was launched into orbit on March 12, 2026, aboard a Long March 8 rocket from the Wenchang Space Launch Center. The constellation, a collaborative project between the China Academy of Space Technology and leading universities including Tsinghua, Peking University, and Jilin University, envisions a network of over 100 low-Earth-orbit satellites that collectively form a space-based computing grid.\n\nUnlike traditional communication satellites that merely relay data, Tiansuan-1 carries onboard high-performance processors capable of performing complex computations in orbit. The satellite is designed to provide real-time data processing services for applications ranging from disaster monitoring to autonomous shipping navigation. Dr. Li Hongfeng, chief scientist of the project, explained: \"Ground-based data centers face limitations in latency, coverage, and energy consumption. A space-based computing network can process data at the edge—closer to where it is generated—enabling services that ground infrastructure alone cannot deliver.\"\n\nThe constellation will be deployed in three phases: Phase 1 (2026–2027) will establish a 30-satellite demonstration network; Phase 2 (2027–2029) will expand coverage to 72 satellites; and Phase 3 (2029–2031) will complete the full 108-satellite constellation. When fully operational, the system is expected to provide computing coverage to over 95 percent of the Earth's surface, including remote areas currently underserved by ground-based infrastructure. International observers have noted that the Space Computing Constellation represents a new paradigm in space utilization, potentially reshaping the competitive landscape of global cloud computing. 33.  What was the key achievement of the Long March 10 rocket test in May 2026?",
