            "transcript": "In 2026, China's consumer goods trade-in program has emerged as one of the most significant economic stimulus measures of the decade. According to data released by the Ministry of Commerce in July 2026, the program drove a total of 1.1 trillion yuan in retail sales during the first half of the year, with over 150 million consumers participating nationwide.\n\nThe policy, which was first introduced in 2024 and significantly expanded in early 2026, covers a wide range of products including household appliances, automobiles, furniture, and electronic devices. Consumers who trade in their old products for new, more energy-efficient models receive direct subsidies ranging from 10% to 20% of the purchase price. For new energy vehicles, the subsidy can reach up to 30,000 yuan per vehicle, while old appliance buybacks typically offer between 200 and 1,000 yuan depending on the item and its condition.\n\nThe program's impact extends beyond mere sales figures. Environmental analysts estimate that the initiative has prevented approximately 8 million tons of electronic waste from entering landfills, as traded-in products are directed to certified recycling facilities. Furthermore, the policy has stimulated technological upgrading across manufacturing sectors, with companies investing heavily in R&D to produce more energy-efficient models that qualify for the subsidy program—creating a virtuous cycle of innovation and consumption.\n\nQuestion 9: How much in total retail sales did the trade-in program generate in the first half of 2026?\nQuestion 10: What is the maximum subsidy available for a new energy vehicle under the program?\nQuestion 11: According to the passage, what additional environmental benefit has the program achieved?",
