        "passage": "In the first half of 2026, China's consumer goods trade-in program demonstrated that economic stimulus and environmental sustainability can indeed go hand in hand. The policy, which offers government subsidies to consumers who replace old products with new, energy-efficient alternatives, generated 1.1 trillion yuan in retail sales— accounting for roughly 4.6% of total retail sales of consumer goods during the period.\n\nThe mechanics are straightforward: consumers bring in used items—be it a ten-year-old refrigerator, a gasoline- powered scooter, or an outdated smartphone—and receive a discount on a qualifying replacement. For major home appliances, the subsidy typically covers 15% of the new product's price, capped at 2,000 yuan per item. The automobile sector sees even more generous terms, with new energy vehicle trade-ins eligible for up to 30,000 yuan in combined central and local subsidies.\n\nThe policy's success owes much to its streamlined implementation. Unlike earlier subsidy programs that required consumers to navigate complex paperwork across multiple government departments, the 2026 version integrates the entire process into major e-commerce platforms. Consumers can verify their eligibility, schedule pickup of old items, and claim their subsidy all within a single app. This reduction in \"hassle cost,\" economists note, has dramatically improved participation rates.\n\nPerhaps the most significant, if less immediately visible, achievement lies in the program's environmental dimension. The Ministry of Ecology and Environment estimates that the trade-in program diverted approximately 8 million tons of electronic and household waste from landfills in the first six months of 2026. Traded-in products are routed to over 300 government-certified recycling facilities, where valuable materials—rare earth metals from electronics, steel from appliances, batteries from vehicles—are extracted and returned to the manufacturing supply chain.\n\nCritics, however, point to the program's long-term sustainability. \"What happens when the subsidies end?\" asks Professor Zhang Wei of Renmin University. \"If consumers have been trained to expect 15–20% discounts as the norm, the market may struggle to maintain momentum once government support is withdrawn.\" Policymakers are reportedly exploring a phased approach to subsidy reduction that would extend through 2028, allowing the market time to adjust. 28. What proportion of total retail sales did the trade-in program account for in the first half of 2026?",
