        "passage": "How Sustainability Is Reshaping Modern Lifestyles—and the Economy That Drives Them\n\nThe summer of 2026 may well be remembered as the season when green consumption in China crossed a critical threshold—from a niche preference of the environmentally conscious few to a mainstream force reshaping industries, supply chains, and everyday habits. The numbers tell a compelling story: sales of \"China Green Product\" certified goods surged 67% year-on-year between June and August; searches for sustainability-related terms on major e- commerce platforms jumped 214%; and the refurbished electronics market expanded by 89% as a new generation of consumers embraced circular economy principles.\n\nBeneath these headline figures lies a more complex and, in many ways, more interesting transformation. What distinguishes the 2026 green consumption wave from earlier, more tentative shifts is not merely its scale but its depth—the degree to which sustainable choices are being integrated into the fabric of daily decision-making rather than treated as occasional, performative gestures.\n\nConsider the changing profile of the green consumer. A 2024 survey by the China Consumers Association found that the typical environmentally conscious shopper was likely to be female, urban, aged 28–40, and relatively affluent. By August 2026, that profile had expanded dramatically. The association's most recent survey of 12,000 respondents across 30 cities found significant growth in green consumption among male consumers (up 23 percentage points), residents of third- and fourth-tier cities (up 31 points), and consumers aged 45–60 (up 18 points). In other words, green consumption was democratizing.\n\nThe motivations behind this shift are equally revealing. While environmental concern remains the most cited driver—62% of respondents identified \"protecting the planet\" as a primary motivation—pragmatic considerations are gaining ground. Forty-seven percent said they chose green products because of perceived health benefits (chemical-free materials, better air quality from energy-efficient appliances), while 41% cited long-term cost savings (lower electricity bills, more durable goods). This convergence of ethical and practical motivations suggests that green consumption is not a temporary trend dependent on moral sentiment but a rational response to tangible benefits.\n\nThe economic ripple effects are substantial. China's green product certification system, which covered approximately 8,000 product categories in 2024, expanded to over 14,000 categories by mid-2026. Manufacturers that had initially resisted the costs of certification are now competing aggressively for green credentials, recognizing that the \"green premium\"—the additional amount consumers will pay for certified sustainable products—can reach 25% in categories such as baby products, cosmetics, and premium home appliances.\n\nYet the path forward is not without obstacles. The most significant of these is greenwashing: the practice of making unsubstantiated or misleading claims about a product's environmental benefits. In the first eight months of 2026, the State Administration for Market Regulation investigated 2,847 cases of suspected greenwashing, a 76% increase from the same period in 2025. Common tactics included vague claims like \"eco-friendly\" or \"natural\" without supporting certification, the use of green-colored packaging to imply environmental benefits where none existed, and selective disclosure—for example, highlighting a single sustainable ingredient while obscuring environmentally harmful manufacturing processes.\n\nAddressing greenwashing will require a multi-pronged approach. Regulators are developing stricter definitions of what constitutes a legitimate green claim, with mandatory third-party verification expected to be phased in by 2028. Consumer education campaigns are teaching shoppers to look beyond marketing language and check for government certification labels. And a growing number of independent watchdog organizations are using artificial intelligence tools to scan product listings at scale, flagging suspicious claims for regulatory review. The green consumption revolution, it seems, is entering its more mature—and more demanding—phase. 38. What does the author mean by saying green consumption \"crossed a critical threshold\" in summer 2026?",
