            "answer": "B",
            "explanation": "细节理解题。问题问：根据文章，什么是厄尔尼诺现象？根据原文 “El Niño... is the warm phase of the El Niño–Southern Oscillation (ENSO) cycle. It occurs when... a pool of anomalously warm water to accumulate along the equatorial Pacific” (厄尔尼诺是厄尔尼诺-南方涛动循环的暖相位。当异常温暖 的海水聚集在赤道太平洋沿岸时发生)，可知B项“一种涉及赤道东太平洋海水异常变暖的气候模式”正确。",
            "transcript": "In August 2026, the World Meteorological Organization officially declared that El Niño conditions had become established in the tropical Pacific Ocean, marking the beginning of what climate models suggest could be one of the strongest El Niño events since the \"super El Niño\" of 2015–2016.\n\nEl Niño, a naturally occurring climate pattern characterized by the unusual warming of surface waters in the eastern equatorial Pacific, occurs every two to seven years and typically lasts nine to twelve months. However, its impacts are anything but local. By altering atmospheric circulation patterns on a global scale, El Niño tends to raise worldwide average temperatures, redistribute rainfall, and increase the frequency and intensity of extreme weather events.\n\nThe WMO's August 2026 assessment projected an 85% probability that the current El Niño would persist through at least March 2027. Sea surface temperature anomalies in the Niño-3.4 monitoring region had reached 1.9°C above the long-term average by late July, a level not observed this early in the development cycle since 1997.\n\nScientists are particularly concerned about the compounding effect of El Niño on top of the long-term global warming trend. Global average temperatures in 2025 were already approximately 1.45°C above pre-industrial levels. The added warming from El Niño could temporarily push global temperatures beyond the symbolic 1.5°C threshold established in the Paris Agreement — not as a permanent breach, but as a foreboding preview of what a 1.5°C world feels like.\n\nThe agricultural implications are especially worrying. El Niño typically brings drought conditions to Southeast Asia, Australia, and parts of India, while causing excessive rainfall and flooding in the Horn of Africa and southern United States. Global grain markets have already responded, with wheat futures rising 18% since June on expectations of reduced harvests in Australia and Argentina.\n\nQuestion 12: According to the passage, what is El Niño?\nQuestion 13: What was the sea surface temperature anomaly in the Niño-3.4 region by late July 2026?\nQuestion 14: What particular concern is raised about the combination of El Niño and long-term global warming?",
            "translation": "2026年8月，世界气象组织正式宣布热带太平洋已形成厄尔尼诺现象，这标志着气候模型所显示的、可能是自2015至2016年“超级厄尔尼诺”以来最强厄尔尼诺事件之一的开始。\n\n厄尔尼诺是一种自然发生的气候型态，其特点是赤道东太平洋表层海水异常增暖，每隔两到七年出现一次，通常持续九到十二个月。然而，它的影响绝非局地性的。通过在全球尺度上改变大气环流型态，厄尔尼诺往往会推高全球平均气温、重新分配降水，并增加极端天气事件的频率和强度。\n\n世界气象组织2026年8月的评估预计，当前厄尔尼诺现象持续到至少2027年3月的概率为85%。到7月下旬，Niño-3.4监测区的海表温度距平已达到比长期平均值高出1.9℃，这是自1997年以来在发展周期中这么早出现如此高值的水平。\n\n科学家尤其担心厄尔尼诺叠加在长期全球变暖趋势之上的复合效应。2025年全球平均气温已比工业化前水平高出约1.45℃。厄尔尼诺带来的额外增温可能暂时将全球气温推过《巴黎协定》设定的象征性1.5℃阈值——这并非永久突破，而是一个令人警醒的预演，让我们感受1.5℃的世界是什么样子。\n\n农业方面的影响尤其令人担忧。厄尔尼诺通常给东南亚、澳大利亚和印度部分地区带来干旱，同时在非洲之角和美国南部造成过量降雨和洪涝。全球谷物市场已经作出反应，自6月以来小麦期货上涨了18%，因为市场预期澳大利亚和阿根廷将减产。\n\nQuestion 12: 根据短文，什么是厄尔尼诺？\nQuestion 13: 到2026年7月下旬，Niño-3.4监测区的海表温度距平是多少？\nQuestion 14: 关于厄尔尼诺与长期全球变暖的结合，短文提出了什么特别的担忧？"
