        "passage": "In August 2026, the World Meteorological Organization (WMO) formally confirmed that El Niño conditions had developed in the tropical Pacific, setting the stage for what climate models project as one of the most potent El Niño events in a generation. For a world already grappling with the consequences of 1.45°C of warming above pre- industrial levels, the arrival of a strong El Niño represents not merely an additional weather perturbation but a stress test of the global capacity to anticipate, prepare for, and respond to climate-driven crises.\n\nEl Niño — Spanish for \"the boy,\" a reference to the Christ child because the phenomenon was historically observed around Christmas by Peruvian fishermen — is the warm phase of the El Niño–Southern Oscillation (ENSO) cycle. It occurs when trade winds that normally push warm surface waters westward across the Pacific weaken or reverse, allowing a pool of anomalously warm water to accumulate along the equatorial Pacific, particularly off the coast of Peru and Ecuador. This redistribution of ocean heat disrupts atmospheric circulation patterns worldwide, producing what the WMO describes as \"a reorganization of global weather.\"\n\nThe 2026 event has developed with alarming speed. By late July, sea surface temperature anomalies in the Niño- 3.4 monitoring region — a rectangular area in the central equatorial Pacific considered the canonical ENSO index region — reached 1.9°C above the long-term mean. This reading is significant not only for its magnitude but for its timing: such a strong anomaly has not been recorded at this stage of the development cycle since the 1997–1998 event, which remains the benchmark for extreme El Niño episodes.\n\nThe cascading consequences are already being felt. In Southeast Asia, including major rice-producing regions of Thailand and Vietnam, rainfall during the 2026 monsoon season has been 30–40% below average, threatening yields at a time when global food prices are already elevated. Australia's Bureau of Meteorology has warned of heightened bushfire risk for the upcoming Southern Hemisphere summer, drawing uncomfortable parallels to the catastrophic \"Black Summer\" fires of 2019–2020. In the Horn of Africa — Ethiopia, Somalia, and Kenya — where communities are still recovering from the devastating 2020–2023 drought, the expected shift to above-normal rainfall brings the paradoxical threat of floods and landslides.\n\nPerhaps most concerning is the potential for El Niño 2026 to temporarily push global average temperatures beyond the 1.5°C threshold that the Paris Agreement identified as a critical guardrail. The WMO's August update estimated a 66% likelihood that at least one year between 2026 and 2028 would exceed 1.5°C of warming. Dr. Petteri Taalas, the WMO Secretary-General, cautioned: \"This is not to say that the Paris target will be permanently breached — that requires sustained warming over a multi-decade average — but it will provide a glimpse of the world we are trying to avoid.\"\n\nThe El Niño of 2026 thus serves as both a warning and an imperative: a warning that the margin for error in the global climate system is narrowing, and an imperative to accelerate investments in adaptation, early warning systems, and the transition away from fossil fuels before the \"glimpse\" becomes a permanent reality. 33. What is the primary purpose of the passage?",
