        "title": "Governing Heat in the Anthropocene: Toward a Global Framework for Coping with Normalized Extremes",
        "passage": "Toward a Global Framework for Coping with Normalized Extremes\n\nIf the summer of 2026 has taught the international community anything, it is that the governance structures through which humanity confronts extreme heat are not remotely commensurate with the scale and complexity of the challenge. As temperature records tumbled across six continents — 35.1°C in a British spring, 48.2°C on the Indian plains, 47.8°C in an American desert city — the accumulated evidence made one thing unmistakably clear: what was once \"extreme\" is rapidly becoming routine, and the policy architecture designed for a cooler, more stable climate is buckling under the strain.\n\nThe governance gap reveals itself at multiple levels. At the local level, the most immediate manifestation is inadequate infrastructure. Most of the world's cities were designed and built for the climate of the 20th century, not the climate of the 21st. Urban heat island effects — whereby dense concentrations of concrete, asphalt, and waste heat from buildings and vehicles raise city temperatures 3–8°C above surrounding rural areas — compound the problem. In Delhi, where 16 consecutive days above 44°C overwhelmed emergency services in June 2026, satellite measurements showed that surface temperatures in densely built neighborhoods reached 56°C on the hottest afternoons — conditions under which outdoor labor becomes physiologically impossible and even indoor spaces without active cooling become hazardous. The city had fewer than 200 public cooling centers for a metropolitan population of 33 million.\n\nAt the national level, the challenge is one of institutional fragmentation. Heat governance — to the extent that it exists at all — is typically dispersed across health ministries, meteorological agencies, urban planning departments, labor regulators, and emergency management bodies, with little coordination among them. A 2026 review published in the journal Climate Policy examined the heat preparedness frameworks of 40 countries and found that only 11 had formally designated \"heat officers\" with cross-departmental authority, and only seven had established legally enforceable workplace heat standards. In the United States, despite heat being the leading cause of weather-related mortality — exceeding hurricanes, floods, and tornadoes combined — the Occupational Safety and Health Administration had still not promulgated a federal heat standard as of mid-2026, leaving workers in agriculture, construction, and warehousing dependent on the uneven protections offered by a patchwork of state-level rules.\n\nAt the international level, the governance vacuum is even more pronounced. Unlike greenhouse gas emissions, which are the subject of a comprehensive (albeit imperfect) multilateral treaty framework in the form of the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change and the Paris Agreement, there exists no equivalent international mechanism dedicated to heat adaptation and resilience. The Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction offers some guidance, but its treatment of extreme heat is cursory at best. The UN Secretary-General's July 2026 call for a \"Global Heat Action Agenda\" — including universal access to early warning systems, standardized heat-health protocols, and dedicated financing for urban cooling infrastructure in developing countries — represents one of the first serious efforts to address this institutional deficit, but whether member states will translate rhetoric into binding commitments remains uncertain.\n\nThe path forward demands a fundamental reconceptualization. Extreme heat can no longer be treated as an exceptional circumstance to be managed with ad hoc measures — opening cooling centers when temperatures spike, issuing warnings when danger has already arrived. Instead, it must be embedded into the permanent fabric of governance: building codes that mandate passive cooling design, urban planning that prioritizes green spaces and reflective surfaces, labor laws that recognize heat as an occupational hazard, and international financing mechanisms that recognize heat resilience as a global public good. The summer of 2026 will not be the last of its kind. Whether it serves as a catalyst for systemic reform or merely another data point in a worsening trajectory depends on the choices governments make in its aftermath. 38. The author's central argument in this passage is that:",
