      "title": "Exercise and Mental Health: The Science Behind the Mind-Body Connection",
      "passage": "For decades, the medical establishment treated physical health and mental health as if they occupied entirely separate domains — a __18__ that contemporary neuroscience has shown to be not only artificial but deeply counterproductive. A __19__ body of evidence now demonstrates that regular physical activity is among the most effective interventions available for both preventing and __20__ symptoms of depression, anxiety, and stress-related disorders.\n\nAt the biological level, exercise exerts its effects through multiple pathways. Physical activity stimulates the release of endorphins, serotonin, and dopamine — neurotransmitters that __21__ mood, pleasure, and motivation. Equally important, regular exercise __22__ the body's cortisol response to stress, meaning that physically active individuals show a significantly smaller stress-hormone spike when faced with psychological challenges compared to their __23__ counterparts. Over time, chronic stress — when left unchecked — can __24__ depressive symptoms, potentially pushing someone from a state of mild __25__ into a full- blown major depressive episode.\n\nThe psychological benefits of exercise are equally profound. Physical activity provides a sense of mastery and self-efficacy that can counteract the feelings of helplessness and __26__ that characterize depression. Group exercise, in particular, combines the biochemical benefits of physical activity with social connection, directly addressing the social withdrawal and isolation that frequently accompany mood disorders. For some patients, this dual effect is powerful enough to __27__ the need for medication — not by masking symptoms but by addressing their underlying biochemical and psychosocial roots.\n\nDespite this evidence, fewer than 15% of mental health practitioners routinely incorporate exercise into their treatment plans. Bridging this gap between research and clinical practice represents one of the most promising frontiers in contemporary mental health care.",
