        "title": "AI-Powered Digital Technologies Become a New Engine for Mental Health Services in China",
        "passage": "In March 2026, the Third National Social Psychological Service Innovation and Development Conference convened in Beijing, drawing over 2,000 experts under the banner \"AI + Internet + Psychological Services.\" The conference marked a pivotal moment in China's response to what the WHO has labeled a \"global mental health crisis\" — one that the pandemic both deepened and thrust into public consciousness.\n\nThe statistics are stark. The China Mental Health Survey found that approximately 18.5% of Chinese adults — nearly 200 million people — experienced a diagnosable mental health condition in the preceding year, yet fewer than 15% received professional care. The bottleneck is structural: China has roughly 40,000 licensed clinical psychologists, yielding a ratio of one per 35,000 citizens. At this capacity, most people who need help simply cannot access it through conventional channels.\n\nAI-based solutions are emerging to fill this gap. Several platforms demonstrated at the conference showed that large language models, trained on clinical datasets and supervised by experienced therapists, can conduct initial screening interviews with accuracy approaching that of junior clinicians. A system developed at Tsinghua University — already deployed in 12 provinces — had processed 2.3 million consultations by March 2026, identifying 87% of cases requiring professional intervention while providing evidence-based self-help resources to those with milder symptoms.\n\nCrucially, developers and clinicians alike emphasize that AI is not positioned as a replacement for human therapists. \"The therapeutic alliance — the trust-based relationship between therapist and client — remains the single strongest predictor of treatment outcomes across all modalities of psychotherapy,\" noted Dr. Li Xiaoming, the conference chair. \"AI can manage the front door — screening, triage, and psychoeducation — but the deepest healing still requires genuine human presence.\"\n\nThe conference also addressed ethical guardrails. Participants called for mandatory transparency when users interact with AI systems, explicit informed consent for data collection, and a national accreditation framework for AI-based mental health tools. By the conclusion, 14 provinces had announced plans to integrate AI mental health screening into primary care systems by 2027 — a development that, if realized, would represent one of the world's largest deployments of AI in public mental health. 28.  According to the passage, approximately what percentage of Chinese adults with mental health conditions receive professional care?",
