            "transcript": "The 2026 Winter Olympics, officially known as Milano-Cortina 2026, took place from February 6 to 22, bringing the Winter Games back to Italy for the first time since Torino hosted them in 2006. The event was distinctive not only for its stunning Alpine scenery but also for its innovative approach to sustainability and territorial organization.\n\nUnlike traditional Olympics concentrated in a single city, Milano-Cortina 2026 was spread across multiple clusters spanning northern Italy. Milan hosted ice sports — figure skating, speed skating, ice hockey, and curling — in state-of-the-art venues such as the PalaItalia Santa Giulia. Cortina d'Ampezzo, the glamorous resort town in the Dolomites, staged alpine skiing events, while Val di Fiemme hosted Nordic skiing, ski jumping, and the new Olympic discipline of ski mountaineering. Biathlon competitions took place in Anterselva, and the sliding sports — bobsled, luge, and skeleton — were held at the rebuilt Cortina Sliding Centre.\n\nApproximately 2,900 athletes from 93 countries participated, competing across 116 medal events. An important milestone was achieved in gender equality: for the first time in Winter Olympic history, female athletes accounted for 47% of all participants — the highest proportion ever recorded.\n\nThe organizing committee placed sustainability at the core of its operations. An impressive 93% of competition venues were either existing or temporary structures, dramatically reducing the construction costs and environmental footprint that have plagued previous Games. The Olympic Village in Milan was designed to be converted into affordable student housing after the Games, while the athletes' accommodations in Cortina were integrated into existing hotel infrastructure.\n\nQuestion 12: What was a distinctive organizational feature of Milano-Cortina 2026?\nQuestion 13: According to the passage, what was achieved in terms of gender equality at these Games?\nQuestion 14: How did the organizers address sustainability concerns?",
            "translation": "2026年冬奥会，正式名称为\"米兰-科尔蒂纳2026\"，于2月6日至22日举行，这是自2006年都灵承办冬奥会以来，冬奥会首次重返意大利。本届赛事不仅以壮丽的阿尔卑斯山风光著称，还以其在可持续性和赛区组织方面的创新做法而独具特色。\n\n与集中在一座城市的传统奥运会不同，米兰-科尔蒂纳2026分布在横跨意大利北部的多个赛区。米兰承办冰上项目——花样滑冰、速度滑冰、冰球和冰壶——场馆包括帕拉意大利圣朱利亚体育馆等最先进的设施。多尔米蒂山脉中迷人的度假小镇科尔蒂纳丹佩佐承办高山滑雪项目，而瓦尔迪菲耶梅则承办北欧滑雪、跳台滑雪以及新增的奥运项目滑雪登山。冬季两项比赛在安泰塞尔瓦举行，雪车、雪橇和钢架雪车等滑行项目则在重建的科尔蒂纳雪车雪橇中心举行。\n\n来自93个国家的约2900名运动员参赛，角逐116个小项的奖牌。性别平等方面实现了一个重要里程碑：在冬奥会历史上，女性运动员首次占全部参赛者的47%——创下历史最高比例。\n\n组委会将可持续性置于其运营的核心。高达93%的比赛场馆为现有或临时建筑，大幅降低了困扰往届奥运会的建设成本和环境足迹。米兰的奥运村被设计为在赛后改造成平价学生公寓，而科尔蒂纳的运动员住宿则融入了现有的酒店设施。\n\nQuestion 12: 米兰-科尔蒂纳2026冬奥会在组织方面有什么独特之处？\nQuestion 13: 根据短文，本届冬奥会在性别平等方面取得了什么成就？\nQuestion 14: 组委会是如何应对可持续性问题的？"
