            "transcript": "In the summer of 2026, a modestly budgeted Chinese film titled \"A Love Letter to Grandma\" (《给阿嬷的情 书》) became an unexpected cultural phenomenon, grossing over 1.5 billion yuan at the domestic box office and igniting a wave of cultural tourism that has drawn the attention of economists and cultural policymakers alike.\n\nThe film, directed by first-time feature filmmaker Chen Yuning, tells the story of a young urban professional who returns to his rural hometown after his grandmother falls ill. Through a series of flashbacks triggered by old letters, photographs, and family recipes, the protagonist rediscovers the quiet wisdom and unconditional love his grandmother embodied — a love he had taken for granted in his pursuit of career success in the city. Critics praised the film's understated performances, its lush cinematography of the Fujian countryside, and its refusal to resort to sentimental manipulation.\n\nHowever, the film's most surprising impact occurred off-screen. The village where it was filmed — a 600-year- old settlement in Fujian Province known for its well-preserved Ming-dynasty architecture and a centuries-old banyan tree — saw its daily visitor numbers surge from approximately 300 to over 8,000 within two months of the film's release. Local homestays reported occupancy rates of 98% during the summer season, and the county's tourism revenue for June–August 2026 tripled compared to the same period in 2025.\n\nThis phenomenon has been hailed as a textbook example of the \"film + cultural tourism\" consumption model that China's cultural authorities have been actively promoting. Unlike fleeting social media-driven tourism fads, the film created a deep emotional connection between audiences and the location, transforming the village from a mere backdrop into a character in its own right. The local government responded by developing heritage walking routes, establishing a film museum, and launching a \"grandmother's kitchen\" culinary experience — all designed to sustain interest beyond the initial wave. By September 2026, over 30 similar film-tourism partnerships had been announced across China, suggesting that \"A Love Letter to Grandma\" may have launched not just a box office success but an enduring economic model. social media tourism trends?\n\nQuestion 15: What is the film \"A Love Letter to Grandma\" about?\nQuestion 16: How did the film affect tourism at its filming location?\nQuestion 17: According to the passage, what distinguishes this \"film + cultural tourism\" model from short-lived",
            "translation": "2026年夏天，一部小成本中国电影《给阿嬷的情书》意外成为文化现象，国内票房突破15亿元，并掀起一股文化旅游热潮，引起了经济学家和文化政策制定者的共同关注。\n\n该片由首次执导长片的导演陈雨凝执导，讲述了一位都市青年白领在奶奶病倒后返回农村老家的故事。通过一封封旧信、老照片和家传菜谱触发的一系列闪回，主人公重新发现了奶奶身上那种沉静的智慧和无条件的爱——一份他在城市里追逐事业成功时曾习以为常的爱。影评人称赞该片内敛克制的表演、对福建乡村风光的唯美呈现，以及不靠煽情来博取观众眼泪的克制。\n\n然而，这部电影最令人意外的冲击发生在银幕之外。取景地是福建省一座有600年历史的古村落，以保存完好的明代建筑和一棵百年古榕树闻名。电影上映两个月内，该村日游客量从约300人激增到8000多人。当地民宿在夏季的入住率达到98%，该县2026年6月至8月的旅游收入比2025年同期增长了两倍。\n\n这一现象被誉为中国文化部门积极推广的\"电影+文旅\"消费模式的典范。与社交媒体驱动的昙花一现式旅游热潮不同，这部电影在观众与取景地之间建立了深厚的情感联结，把村庄从单纯的背景板变成了影片中一个有血有肉的角色。当地政府顺势推出了文化遗产徒步路线，建立了电影博物馆，并推出了\"阿嬷厨房\"美食体验——一切都是为了在首波热潮过后继续留住游客。到2026年9月，全国已宣布30多个类似的电影文旅合作项目，这表明《给阿嬷的情书》或许不仅缔造了票房佳绩，还开启了一种持久的经济模式。\n\nQuestion 15: 电影《给阿嬷的情书》讲述了什么？\nQuestion 16: 这部电影对取景地的旅游业产生了什么影响？\nQuestion 17: 根据短文，这种\"电影+文旅\"模式与昙花一现的社交媒体旅游热潮有何区别？"
