        "passage": "The year 2026 has been described by sports economists as the most densely packed year of major international sporting events in modern history — a \"super sports season\" that captivated billions and reshaped the global sports calendar.\n\nThe year began with the Milano-Cortina Winter Olympics in February, which drew a cumulative global television audience of 2.8 billion viewers across 16 days of competition. Italy leveraged the Games to showcase its northern regions, with the Lombardy and Veneto tourism boards reporting a 34% increase in international arrivals during the first quarter compared to 2025.\n\nOnly four months later, attention shifted across the Atlantic. The 2026 FIFA World Cup kicked off on June 11, spanning 16 cities across the United States, Canada, and Mexico. With 48 teams — up from the traditional 32 — and 104 matches, it was the largest World Cup ever staged. FIFA projected a combined in-stadium attendance of 5.8 million and a global broadcast audience exceeding 5 billion. The host nations poured an estimated $6.2 billion into infrastructure upgrades, including stadium renovations, airport expansions, and public transit improvements.\n\nSandwiched between these two giants was an event of immense regional significance: the 2026 Asian Games in Aichi-Nagoya, Japan, scheduled for September 19 to October 4. While smaller in global profile than the Olympics or World Cup, the Asian Games represented a critical milestone for Japan, marking the country's first time hosting the event since Hiroshima in 1994. With 42 nations participating across 40 sports, including the debut of esports as a full medal event, the Aichi-Nagoya Games were projected to generate ¥540 billion ($3.6 billion) in economic activity for the Chubu region.\n\nThe convergence of these three mega-events in a single calendar year has no precedent. Analysts at Deloitte's Sports Business Group estimated that the combined economic impact of the 2026 Winter Olympics, World Cup, and Asian Games would reach $18.5 billion across host nations — a figure that dwarfs any previous sports year. However, the clustering of events also raised questions about bidding competition for future cycles, athlete welfare amid compressed qualification schedules, and the environmental sustainability of a model that demands ever-larger investments from host cities and nations. 28. According to the passage, what was the cumulative global TV audience for the Milano-Cortina Winter Olympics?",
