        "title": "From Screen to Street: How \"A Love Letter to Grandma\" Rewrote the Rules of Film-Driven Cultural Tourism in China",
        "passage": "Tourism in China\n\nWhen director Chen Yuning wrapped filming on \"A Love Letter to Grandma\" in late 2025, the production's total budget stood at 48 million yuan — a modest figure by contemporary Chinese cinema standards, where blockbusters routinely exceed 300 million yuan in production costs alone. No one involved in the project anticipated what would unfold in the summer of 2026.\n\nThe film was released on June 12, 2026, and by its sixth weekend, it had accumulated 1.52 billion yuan in box office receipts, making it the highest-grossing non-franchise, non-animated Chinese film of the year. But the numbers that truly astonished industry observers were not on the balance sheets of cinema chains — they were on the ledgers of village guesthouses, rural restaurants, and local transport operators in a corner of Fujian Province that had, until then, been largely absent from tourist maps.\n\nThe village of Shicuo, where the film was shot, received an average of 300 visitors per day in May 2026. By July, the daily count had surged to 8,200, peaking at 14,000 on weekends. The local government's tourism bureau reported that the county's total tourism revenue for the June–August quarter reached ¥2.1 billion — a 198% increase from ¥705 million in the same period of 2025. The number of registered homestays in the county grew from 47 to 186 within three months, and the average nightly rate rose from ¥180 to ¥520.\n\nWhat made this case distinctive was not the scale of the tourism surge — social media platforms have periodically fueled similar booms for photogenic locations — but its apparent durability. Unlike the \"check-in\" tourism driven by short-video platforms, where visitors arrive, take photographs for social media, and depart within hours, the visitors drawn by \"A Love Letter to Grandma\" tended to stay longer and spend more. A survey by the Fujian Provincial Department of Culture and Tourism found that 63% of film-motivated visitors stayed at least two nights, and 71% reported that emotional engagement with the film's story — rather than the aesthetic appeal of the scenery alone — was their primary motivation for visiting. This emotional anchoring, researchers argued, transformed passive sightseers into active cultural participants.\n\nThe commercial ecosystem that emerged around the film extended well beyond tourism. A line of licensed kitchenware inspired by the grandmother character's cooking scenes generated ¥340 million in sales. The film's soundtrack, featuring traditional Fujianese folk melodies reinterpreted by a contemporary composer, spent eight weeks at number one on China's music streaming charts. And in a sign of the model's replicability, by October 2026, provincial governments in Yunnan, Guizhou, and Sichuan had each announced incentive programs offering tax rebates and location subsidies to film productions that committed to shooting in underserved rural areas — a direct policy response to the Shicuo phenomenon. 33.  What was the production budget of \"A Love Letter to Grandma\"?",
