        "passage": "Perfectionism, the relentless striving for flawlessness and the setting of excessively high-performance standards, is often viewed as a desirable trait in today's competitive society. However, psychological research has increasingly revealed that this drive for perfection can be a double-edged sword, often leading to significant distress and dysfunction. It is important to distinguish between healthy striving for excellence and maladaptive perfectionism.\n\nHealthy strivers, or \"adaptive perfectionists,\" set high but attainable goals. They are motivated by a desire for achievement and personal growth. When they fall short of their goals, they are able to be self-compassionate, learn from their mistakes, and adjust their efforts. This form of perfectionism is associated with high levels of motivation, conscientiousness, and success.\n\nIn contrast, \"maladaptive perfectionists\" are driven by a fear of failure and a constant need for approval. They set impossibly high standards and are overly critical of their own performance. For them, anything less than perfect is a complete failure. This leads to a chronic state of anxiety, procrastination, and burnout. They often avoid taking on new challenges for fear of not meeting their own impossible expectations.\n\nThe roots of maladaptive perfectionism are complex, often linked to early childhood experiences and societal pressures. It can be a significant risk factor for a range of mental health issues, including depression, anxiety disorders, and eating disorders. Overcoming this form of perfectionism involves learning to challenge unrealistic standards, practicing self-compassion, and redefining success as progress and effort, not just a perfect outcome. The goal is to shift from a rigid, all-or-nothing mindset to a more flexible and self-accepting one. 33. What is the main idea of the passage?",
