        "passage": "The field of aerospace engineering is at the cusp of a new era, driven by the convergence of private enterprise and technological breakthroughs. While government agencies like NASA have historically led space exploration, a new generation of private companies, such as SpaceX and Blue Origin, is now playing an increasingly dominant role, dramatically reducing the cost of access to space and accelerating the pace of innovation.\n\nOne of the most significant developments is the successful development and reuse of orbital-class rockets. Traditionally, rockets were used once and then discarded, making each launch extremely expensive. The ability to land a rocket's first stage on a drone ship or launch pad and reuse it for multiple flights has revolutionized the economics of spaceflight. This breakthrough has made it feasible to launch larger payloads more frequently, opening up possibilities for massive satellite constellations, space tourism, and even missions to Mars.\n\nAnother key area of focus is the development of advanced propulsion systems. While chemical rockets remain the workhorse, new technologies like ion thrusters and nuclear thermal propulsion are being researched. Ion thrusters, which use electricity to accelerate ions, are incredibly fuel-efficient, offering a potential for longer-duration missions with less propellant. Nuclear thermal propulsion, which uses a nuclear reactor to heat propellant, could drastically reduce travel time to distant destinations like Mars, making human missions more viable.\n\nThe future of aerospace is not just about reaching farther destinations; it is also about utilizing space to improve life on Earth. Satellite technology is critical for global communications, weather forecasting, navigation, and Earth observation. As the cost of launch decreases, we can expect to see even more innovative applications, from high- speed global internet coverage to precise monitoring of climate change and natural disasters. The new space race is not just about exploration; it is about building a space-based infrastructure that will fundamentally reshape our global economy and our understanding of our planet. 38. What is the main topic of the passage?",
