          "transcript": "W: Hi, Professor Smith. I hear you've written a book on future trends.\nM: That's right. It's called \"The World in 2050\".\nW: Can you tell us what the world will be like in 2050?\nM: Well, one thing is for sure, the population will be much larger. There will be more than 9 billion people on Earth.\nW: That's a lot of people. Will we have enough food?\nM: I think so. But we'll have to use technology much more efficiently. We'll also have to control the environment better.\nW: What about energy?\nM: We'll have to find new sources of energy. Oil will be running out. I think we'll use more solar and wind power. And maybe we'll even find a way to use nuclear fusion.\nW: Will people live longer?\nM: Yes, definitely. The average life span will be over 80 years. In some countries, it might even be over 90.\nW: Will people work longer too?\nM: I think so. People will have to work longer because there won't be enough young people to support the old.\nW: What about technology? Will we have robots doing all the work?\nM: Not all the work, but many routine jobs will be done by robots. People will have more time for creative activities.\nW: That sounds wonderful. But won't there be problems like unemployment?\nM: There might be some problems, but I think we'll find solutions. The key is to educate people to adapt to the new environment.\nQuestions 22 to 25 are based on the conversation you've just heard.\n22. What does Professor Smith say about people in the past?\n23. What does Professor Smith think people should do to cope with future changes?\n24. When will population stop growing according to Professor Smith?\n25. What is the most important thing we need to do for the future?",
