          "explanation": "细节理解题。问题问：根据短文，全球变暖最可怕的威胁之一是什么？根据听力原文 \"raising sea level so much that coastal cities from New York to Los Angeles to Shanghai will be flooded\" (使海平面升高如此之多，以致从纽约到洛杉矶再到上海的沿海城市都将被淹没)，可知许多沿海城市将被水淹没，C项正确。",
          "transcript": "Among global warming's most frightening threats is the prediction that the polar ice-caps will melt, raising sea level so much that coastal cities from New York to Los Angeles to Shanghai will be flooded. Scientists agree that the key player in this scenario is the West Antarctic ice sheet, a Brazil-size mass of frozen water that is as much as 7,000 feet thick. Unlike floating ice shelves which have little impact on sea level when they break up, the ice sheet is anchored to bedrock and will raise the sea surface. Surrounded by open ocean, it is also vulnerable, but Antarctic experts disagree strongly on just how unstable it is. Now, new evidence reveals that all or most of the Antarctic ice sheet collapsed at least once during the past 1.3 million years, a period when global temperatures probably were not significantly higher than they are today. And the ice sheet was assumed to have been stable. In geological time, a million years is recent history. The proof, which was published last week in Science, comes from a team of scientists from Uppsala University in Sweden and California Institute of Technology who drilled deep holes near the edge of ice sheet, within samples collected from the solid substances lying beneath the ice. They found fossils of microscopic marine plants which suggest that the region was once open ocean not solid ice. As Herman Engelheart, a co-author from the California Institute of Technology says, \"The West Antarctic ice sheet disappeared once and can disappear again.\"",
          "translation": "在全球变暖最令人恐惧的威胁中，有一种预测是极地冰盖将会融化，使海平面大幅上升，以至于从纽约到洛杉矶再到上海的沿海城市都会被淹没。科学家们一致认为，这一情景中的关键因素是南极洲西部的冰盖，这是一块面积相当于巴西、厚达7000英尺的冰冻水体。与破裂时对海平面影响很小的漂浮冰架不同，这块冰盖固定在基岩上，会使海面上升。它四周被开阔海洋环绕，因此也很脆弱，但南极专家对它到底有多不稳定存在激烈分歧。现在，新的证据显示，南极冰盖的全部或大部分在过去130万年里至少崩塌过一次，而那个时期的全球气温可能并不比今天高多少。人们此前一直以为这块冰盖是稳定的。以地质年代来衡量，一百万年只是很近的历史。这项证据上周发表在《科学》杂志上，来自瑞典乌普萨拉大学和加州理工学院的一个科学家团队，他们在冰盖边缘附近钻了深孔，从冰下的固体物质中采集了样本。他们发现了微小的海洋植物化石，表明该地区曾经是开阔的海洋，而不是坚固的冰层。正如来自加州理工学院的合著者赫尔曼·恩格尔哈特所说：“南极洲西部的冰盖曾经消失过，也可能会再次消失。”"
