          "transcript": "Everyone is looking for a good investment these days. And with stocks, currencies and companies all crashing, some are finding that taking the trip of a lifetime is actually a smart move right now. Prices are good, crowds are fewer and the dividends like expanded worldview, lifelong memories, and the satisfaction of boosting the global economy — can't be easily snatched away. Sylvia and Paul Custer son, a retired couple from Cambridge, England, recently took a 16-day vacation to Namibia, where they went on bird-watching excursions. Later this year, they are planning a trip to Patagonia. \"We're using our capital now,\" says Sylvia, \"And why not? We're not getting any interest in the bank. If it's a place we really want to go, then we will go. We may as well travel while we're fit and healthy.\"\n\nSome travel agents are thriving in spite of the economy. \"We've had more people booking in the first quarter of this year than last,\" says Hubert Moniteau, founder of Solana Travel, which is planning to introduce a new program of longer adventure trips, including polar expeditions and cruises in the Galápagos. \"We're hearing things like, 'We don't know what the situation will be in six months so let's travel now'\", Ashley Tuft, managing director of the U.K. tour operator Explore has been surprised to see an increase in last-minute bookings of high-priced trips to such places as India, Bhutan and Nepal. \"It seems people would rather give up something else than the big trip,\" he says. Travel has become a necessity. It's just how we travel that is changing.",
          "translation": "如今人人都在寻找好的投资方式。在股票、货币和公司纷纷暴跌的情况下，有些人发现，来一次终生难忘的旅行其实正是眼下的明智之举。价格合适，人也不那么拥挤，而且开阔眼界、终身难忘的回忆、拉动全球经济的满足感等“红利”也不容易被夺走。来自英格兰剑桥的退休夫妇西尔维娅和保罗·卡斯特森最近去纳米比亚度了16天假，在那里参加了观鸟活动。今年晚些时候，他们计划去巴塔哥尼亚旅行。“我们现在就在花我们的积蓄，”西尔维娅说，“为什么不呢？钱存在银行也拿不到利息。如果那是我们真正想去的地方，那就去。趁着我们还身强力壮，不如多出去走走。”\n\n尽管经济不景气，一些旅行社却生意兴隆。“今年第一季度预订的人数比去年还多，”索拉纳旅行社创始人休伯特·莫尼托说。这家旅行社正计划推出时间更长的探险旅行新项目，包括极地探险和加拉帕戈斯群岛邮轮之旅。“我们听到人们说，‘我们不知道六个月后形势会怎样，所以现在就出发吧’”，英国旅游运营商Explore的总经理阿什利·塔夫特惊讶地发现，去印度、不丹和尼泊尔等地的高价旅行出现了越来越多最后一刻预订的情况。“人们似乎宁愿放弃别的东西，也不愿放弃这趟大旅行，”他说。旅行已成为一种必需品。只是我们的旅行方式正在改变。"
