          "explanation": "细节理解题。问题问：关于拉塞尔·法齐奥我们了解到什么？根据听力原文 \"Russell Fazio, an Ohio State psychology professor who has studied interracial roommates there and at Indiana University\" (拉塞尔·法齐奥是俄亥俄州立大学的心理学教授，研究过跨种族室友间的关系)，可知C项正确。",
          "transcript": "Russell Fazio, an Ohio State psychology professor who has studied interracial roommates there and at Indiana University, discovered an intriguing academic effect. In a study analyzing data on thousands of Ohio State freshmen who lived in dorms, he found that black freshmen who came to college with high standardized test scores earned better grades if they had a white roommate — even if the roommate's test scores were low. The roommate's race had no effect on the grades of white students or low-scoring black students. Perhaps, the study speculated, having a white roommate helps academically prepared black students adjust to a predominantly white university.\n\nThat same study found that randomly assigned interracial roommates at Ohio State broke up before the end of the quarter about twice as often as same-race roommates.\n\nBecause interracial roommate relationships are often problematic, Dr. Fazio said, many students would like to move out, but university housing policies may make it hard to leave.\n\n\"At Indiana University, where housing was not so tight, more interracial roommates split up,\" he said. \"Here at Ohio State, where housing was tight, they were told to work it out. The most interesting thing we found was that if the relationship managed to continue for just 10 weeks, we could see an improvement in racial attitudes.\"\n\nDr. Fazio's Indiana study found that three times as many randomly assigned interracial roommates were no longer living together at the end of the semester, compared with white roommates. The interracial roommates spent less time together, and had fewer joint activities than the white pairs.",
          "translation": "拉塞尔·法齐奥是俄亥俄州立大学的心理学教授，他在该校和印第安纳大学研究过跨种族室友，发现了一种有趣的学业效应。在一项分析数千名住宿舍的俄亥俄州立大学新生数据的研究中，他发现，带着高标准化考试成绩入学的黑人新生，如果有一个白人室友，成绩会更好——即使这位室友的考试成绩很低。室友的种族对白人学生或成绩较低的黑人学生的成绩没有影响。研究推测，或许拥有一位白人室友有助于在学业上准备充分的黑人学生适应以白人为主的大学。\n\n同一项研究还发现，在俄亥俄州立大学，随机分配的跨种族室友在学期结束前分开的比例，大约是同种族室友的两倍。\n\n法齐奥博士说，由于跨种族室友关系常常出现问题，许多学生想要搬出去，但大学的住宿政策可能让他们很难离开。\n\n“在印第安纳大学，住宿不那么紧张，跨种族室友分开的更多，”他说。“在俄亥俄州立大学这里，住宿紧张，学校让他们把问题解决掉。我们发现的最有意思的一点是，如果这段关系能坚持维持10周，我们就能看到种族态度上的改善。”\n\n法齐奥博士在印第安纳大学的研究发现，到学期结束时，随机分配的跨种族室友不再住在一起的数量是白人室友的三倍。跨种族室友在一起的时间更少，共同活动也比白人室友组合更少。"
