          "answer": "A",
          "explanation": "细节理解题。问题问：关于菲利斯·惠特利，说话者说了什么？根据听力原文 \"She was also a poet and the first African-American ever to publish a book\" (她还是一位诗人，也是第一位出版书籍的非裔美国人)，可知A项正确。",
          "transcript": "Phyllis Wheatley was a young African-American slave, who belonged to landowner John Wheatley in Colonial America. She was also a poet and the first African-American ever to publish a book. Her poems on various subjects, religious and moral, were printed in Boston in 1773, three years before the signing of the Declaration of Independence. Early slaves were generally denied education, but Wheatley was allowed by her owner to study poetry, Latin and the Bible. And by the time she reached her late teens, she had written enough poetry to put together a slender book of verse. Even so, publication was difficult. The publisher, fearful of being cheated, forced her to submit to a scholarly examination by a board of educated men, including the colonial governor. The board of judges questioned Wheatley extensively and ruled that she was educated enough to have written the book. Only then was publication permitted. Wheatley may have been the first but she was not the only slave to write a book during the growing days of the Republic. Unfortunately, most of the early popular African-American writers have been all but forgotten in modern times until now. A Cornell professor, Henry Louis Gates, recently directed a research project looking into 19th-century African-American fiction and poetry. In the process, he uncovered numerous lost works, almost half of which were written by African-American women. In varied literary styles, the newly resurfaced manuscripts offered a rich stock of African-American culture, recreating among other things the early days of slavery and the importance of religion to the slaves.",
          "translation": "菲利斯·惠特利是一名年轻的非裔美国奴隶，属于殖民地时期美国的地主约翰·惠特利。她还是一位诗人，是第一位出版书籍的非裔美国人。她关于宗教和道德等各种主题的诗集于1773年在波士顿印刷出版，比《独立宣言》的签署早三年。早期的奴隶通常被剥夺受教育的权利，但惠特利获她的主人允许学习诗歌、拉丁语和《圣经》。到她十八九岁的时候，她已经写了足够多的诗，可以汇编成一本薄薄的诗集。即便如此，出版仍很困难。出版商担心受骗，迫使她接受一个由受过教育的人组成的委员会的学术考查，其中还包括殖民地总督。评审委员会对惠特利进行了广泛提问，并裁定她受过足够的教育，足以写出这本书。直到这时，出版才获得许可。惠特利可能是第一个，但她并不是共和国成长时期唯一写书的奴隶。不幸的是，大多数早期受欢迎的非裔美国作家直到现在都几乎被现代人所遗忘。康奈尔大学教授亨利·路易斯·盖茨最近主持了一个研究项目，考察19世纪的非裔美国小说和诗歌。在此过程中，他发现了大量失落的作品，其中近一半是非裔美国女性创作的。这些重新浮出水面的手稿以多样的文学风格，提供了丰富的非裔美国文化宝库，其中再现了早期的奴隶制生活以及宗教对奴隶的重要性等。"
